From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Berkay=20K=C3=B6sebay?= <brkosebay@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 16:01:53 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] psci: Implement SYSTEM_RESET and SYSTEM_OFF with a CPU
 rendezvous

Give the kernel's ordinary reboot and power-off paths somewhere to go.
On SYSTEM_RESET or SYSTEM_OFF the calling CPU sets the rendezvous flag,
sends an event and an AIC IPI to every other CPU the kernel was given,
and waits up to a second for all of them to park in the pen through the
WFI/WFE traps. If one does not arrive, it clears the flag and returns
without having changed anything, so the kernel goes on to its next
restart or power-off method as it does today.

Once they are parked it disarms the primary watchdog (the kernel, or
systemd's reboot watchdog, may have armed it), stops its timers and
drops the traps. SYSTEM_RESET then turns the EL1 MMU and caches off,
writes back every data cache level by set/way, invalidates the EL1 TLB
and I-cache and restarts m1n1 at EL1h with the original iBoot boot_args,
saved in .data on the first cold start before top_of_memory_alloc()
changes them. psci_reentered() and the new psci_reset_reentered() tell
the restarted m1n1 where it came from. SYSTEM_OFF parks the caller too:
there is no PMU driver here, so this halts rather than powers off.

The payload variable psci_reset selects what SYSTEM_RESET leads to: boot
(the default, used by the next change), proxy, or 0 for NOT_SUPPORTED
with no WFI/WFE traps. It is reported by PSCI_FEATURES and armed by
kboot_boot() through the private handoff call, which also records the
boot CPU and needs an AIC v1 and a watchdog in the ADT.

Assisted-by: LLM
